{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי אתה בא", :translit "ki atah ba", :gloss "for you are coming"} {:he "שואל אוב", :translit "sho'el ov", :gloss "inquirer of an ov"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah (adjacency): the phrase 'כי אתה בא' is placed near the topic of שואל אוב to draw a legal inference about the king."}], :frame {:text "The king of Israel may inquire of God through the Urim veTummim (Numbers 27:21). The prohibition against inquiring of an ov is stated at Deuteronomy 18:11, in close proximity to Deuteronomy 18:9 ('כי אתה בא אל הארץ'). The Talmud derives that specifically the king uses the Urim veTummim, as stated in the Gemara's discussion of the eight matters reserved for the king or the high court.", :sources ["Numbers 27:21" "Sanhedrin 2a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah (adjacency): the phrase 'כי אתה בא' is placed near the topic of שואל אוב to draw a legal inference about the king.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:37:17.790328519Z", :model "glm-5.2"}